The census is complete: 12 instantiation call sites examined, with eight always taken for these flags and up to two IDE calls taken when images exist. One correction to the stepping premise matters: `timer_stop()` prevents new PIT/RTC timer events, but device ticks still run during stepping and can process pending events accumulated earlier.
codex
**VERDICT: Keyboard, both UARTs, and instantiated IDE controllers have synchronous guest-access assertion paths. PIT/IRQ 0 is present; PIT and RTC assertions require host-timer events. An ISA assertion propagated to LCA reaches a TODO handler that exits the emulator.**

This is a static census of `GXEMUL-SEC/src/`. The background checks out: `MACHINE_SETUP(alpha)` creates LCA in the `ST_ALPHABOOK1` case, and `DEVINIT(lca)` passes exactly `BUS_ISA_IDE0 | BUS_ISA_IDE1`. [machine_alpha.c:57](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine_alpha.c:57), [dev_lca.c:385](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:385)

I examined **12 instantiation call sites in `bus_isa_init`: ten `device_add` calls, one `dev_vga_init`, and one `dev_pckbc_init`.** For these flags, **eight calls always execute, plus zero, one, or two WDC calls depending on attached IDE images**. FDC and VGA are omitted. Thus the flags alone do not determine the exact device count. The PIC creation is also flag-dependent in the general function, although both PICs are created here. [bus_isa.c, bus_isa_init](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:174)

The address arithmetic is:

`B = LCA_ISA_BASE = LCA_PCI_SIO + 0x10000000 = 0x1c0000000 + 0x10000000 = 0x1d0000000`.

Every `B+offset` below is therefore the requested machine-specific mapping—for example, UART0 starts at `0x1d00003f8`. [dev_lca.c:47](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:47), [alpha_lcareg.h:45](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/include/thirdparty/alpha_lcareg.h:45)

| Device created | ISA IRQ/output | Guest physical I/O mapping | What produces an assertion |
|---|---|---|---|
| Primary 8259 PIC | Output directly to `<machine>.lca`; handles IRQs 0–7 | `B+0x20`–`B+0x21` | **Synchronous forwarding/recalculation.** In `dev_8259_access`, interrupt-mask writes and EOI command writes can assert when recalculation leaves unmasked pending requests. It does not independently originate an interrupt request. |
| Secondary 8259 PIC | Output to ISA **2**; handles IRQs 8–15 | `B+0xa0`–`B+0xa1` | Same `dev_8259_access` behavior. Neither PIC uses a host timer. [dev_8259.c:103](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_8259.c:103), [mask-write path:211](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_8259.c:211) |
| 8253 PIT | **0** | `B+0x40`–`B+0x43` | Guest counter programming installs/updates a host timer. Its `timer0_tick` increments the pending count; subsequently `dev_8253_tick` asserts when active and pending. **Host-timer-dependent.** [dev_8253.c:109](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_8253.c:109), [timer installation:191](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_8253.c:191) |
| PCCMOS, containing an MC146818 RTC | **8** | `B+0x70` index; `B+0x71` data | Guest RTC register-A rate programming creates/updates the timer; register-B periodic-interrupt enable gates assertion. Host `timer_tick` increments pending; `dev_mc146818_tick` asserts. **Host-timer-dependent.** [dev_mc146818.c:108](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_mc146818.c:108), [register writes:400](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_mc146818.c:400) |
| NS16550 `tty0` | **4** | `B+0x3f8`–`B+0x3ff` | **Synchronous TX-ready or RX-ready assertion is possible.** `dev_ns16550_access` calls `dev_ns16550_tick` on DATA accesses, IER/MCR writes, and IIR reads. Assertion requires the corresponding interrupt enable and the MCR interrupt gate. Console input also supplies RX-ready state during scheduled device ticks. |
| NS16550 `tty1` | **3** | `B+0x2f8`–`B+0x2ff` | Same UART paths. Its `in_use=0` does **not** disable the interrupt logic: `dev_ns16550_tick` does not test `in_use`. [dev_ns16550.c, tick/access](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_ns16550.c:74), [MCR path:282](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_ns16550.c:282) |
| LPT | **7** connected | `B+0x378`–`B+0x37a` | **Never asserts in this implementation.** `dev_lpt_tick` is empty; `dev_lpt_access` stores data/control and outputs a character on a strobe, without asserting IRQ. [dev_lpt.c:61](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lpt.c:61) |
| WDC0, **only if IDE image 0 or 1 exists** | **14** | `B+0x1f0`–`B+0x1f7`; alternate/control region `B+0x3f6`–`B+0x3f7` | **Synchronous command and data-phase assertions.** Guest command/data accesses set `int_assert`; `dev_wdc_access` calls `dev_wdc_tick` before returning. Details below. |
| WDC1, **only if IDE image 2 or 3 exists** | **15** | `B+0x170`–`B+0x177`; alternate/control region `B+0x376`–`B+0x377` | Same WDC paths. Image gating is in `bus_isa_init`; the alternate address is controller base plus `0x206` in `DEVINIT(wdc)`. [bus_isa.c:270](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:270), [dev_wdc.c:1981](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1981) |
| PCKBC, **8042 with PC-style addressing** | **1** reachable; **12** connected but dormant here | Data `B+0x60`; status/command `B+0x64`. Registered region: `B+0x60`–`B+0x6f` | **Synchronous keyboard assertion:** guest keyboard commands and controller tests queue replies; `dev_pckbc_access` finishes by calling `pckbc_reassert_interrupts`. Console input can also queue keyboard scancodes in `dev_pckbc_tick` when the keyboard console is in use. [dev_pckbc.c:606](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pckbc.c:606), [command handling:735](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pckbc.c:735), [access tail:1191](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pckbc.c:1191) |

PCCMOS’s `DEVINIT(pccmos)` additionally calls `dev_mc146818_init`; that is a **nested instantiation**, outside the 12-call denominator. `dev_pccmos_access` forwards selected RTC registers through the helper’s artificial mapping at `0x1d00000000`; the ISA-facing interface remains the index/data pair above. [dev_pccmos.c:57](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pccmos.c:57), [nested initialization:153](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pccmos.c:153)

The LCA sparse I/O window is another entry into these mappings: `dev_lca_isa_access` computes the port offset by shifting its relative address right by five, then forwards to `LCA_ISA_BASE + offset`. `DEVINIT(lca)` registers that window at `LCA_PCI_SIO`. [dev_lca.c:152](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:152), [window registration:364](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:364)

**IRQ 12 is a reachability exclusion, not merely an inactive default.** `dev_pckbc_init` initializes mouse receive interrupts and scanning disabled. In `dev_pckbc_access`, `port_nr` remains zero for this 8042 configuration; only the 8242 branch selects port one. Loading the command byte therefore updates the keyboard receive-enable entry. The auxiliary-command branches also leave `port_nr` unchanged. My static control-flow conclusion is that ordinary accesses through this instantiated interface cannot enable the mouse IRQ12 assertion path. Keyboard replies remain possible even when keyboard console input is unused. [dev_pckbc.c:913](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pckbc.c:913), [command-byte handling:999](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pckbc.c:999), [auxiliary handling:1077](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pckbc.c:1077), [defaults:1230](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_pckbc.c:1230)

For **WDC**, the answer to Q3 is **yes, with a controller instantiated; no OS boot is required by this path**:

`dev_wdc_access` on a **`wd_command` write, controller offset 7**, calls `wdc_command`. After it returns, the access handler directly calls `dev_wdc_tick`, which asserts if `int_assert` is set. Initialization enables controller I/O. These are ordinary calls within the guest store’s handler. [dev_wdc.c:1932](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1932), [inline tick:1955](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1955), [initialization:1993](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1993)

All active setters of `int_assert = 1` are in three functions:

- **`wdc_command`:** terminal command handling—parameter initialization, features, recalibration, identification, idle, set-multiple, ATAPI soft reset, diagnostics, and abort/error branches. [dev_wdc.c:836](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:836)
- **`wdc__read`:** READ/READMULTI processing sets it after the disk-image accesses return. [dev_wdc.c:530](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:530)
- **`dev_wdc_access`, DATA register:** read continuation/ATAPI phase transitions, ATAPI packet processing, and buffered disk-write completion set it. `wdc__write` itself only establishes write state; subsequent DATA stores perform the write and set the latch. [read-side setters:1374](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1374), [ATAPI processing:1660](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1660), [write completion:1776](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:1776), [wdc__write:555](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:555)

Consequently, “disk operation completing” here can occur inside the guest access. The WDC completion paths do not require `timer_add` or SIGALRM.

There are two distinct missing-image cases:

- **Neither image in a controller’s pair exists:** `bus_isa_init` omits that controller entirely, including its WDC register mappings.
- **The controller exists, but the selected drive has no image:** `wdc_command` sets `WDCE_ABRT` **and** `int_assert`, then returns to the inline assertion path. [dev_wdc.c:855](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_wdc.c:855)

**Your Q4 inference is correct about PIT presence.** The explicit creation is at `bus_isa.c:241–244`. Its `in_use=0` parameter means initially inactive: `dev_8253_access` sets `in_use=1`. That access does not manufacture a pending timer event or call the assertion routine; pending events originate in the host callback. [bus_isa.c:241](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:241), [dev_8253.c:132](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_8253.c:132)

The stepping distinction needs **three categories**:

- **Inside a guest load/store:** UART, keyboard command/reply handling, WDC, and PIC recalculation.
- **Scheduled device ticks without a host-timer prerequisite:** UART/keyboard input polling and reassertion of existing WDC state.
- **Host-timer-dependent production, followed by a device tick:** PIT and RTC. Their host callbacks increment pending counters; their device ticks perform the actual assertions.

`machine_run` continues advancing device-tick schedules while single-stepping, explicitly using a decrement of one. Therefore a function named `DEVICE_TICK` is not necessarily a SIGALRM callback. [machine.c, machine_run:726](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine.c:726)

Your debugger observation is correct: `debugger()` stops timers, and the step return precedes `timer_start()`. The qualification is that **stopping timers prevents new PIT/RTC pending events; it does not clear pending events accumulated earlier**. Those can still be processed by device ticks while stepping. This follows from `timer_stop`, the retained device counters, and `machine_run`. [debugger.c:799](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/debugger/debugger.c:799), [step return:834](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/debugger/debugger.c:834), [timer.c, timer_stop:346](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/core/timer.c:346)

Finally, two routing findings matter:

- `isa_interrupt_assert` records the device request in PIC IRR state. `isa_interrupt_common` cascades unmasked secondary requests onto primary IRQ2, applies masks, records `last_int`, and calls the upstream interrupt. Here that upstream handler is **`lca_interrupt_assert`, which prints its TODO diagnostic and calls `exit(1)`**. `lca_interrupt_deassert` does the same. Thus an assertion that propagates upstream terminates there; these handlers contain no CPU interrupt delivery. [bus_isa.c:52](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:52), [assert entry:98](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:98), [dev_lca.c:70](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_lca.c:70)
- The IRQ0 acknowledgement comment also has an initialization-order caveat. `bus_isa_init` copies `pending_timer_interrupts` **before** creating the PIT; `DEVINIT(8253)` installs that pointer afterward. Since `machine_new` zeroes the machine, my static conclusion is that the bus’s copied pointer stays NULL on this path, making that particular deassert-side counter decrement inactive. [bus_isa.c:224](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:224), [dev_8253.c:383](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/dev_8253.c:383), [machine.c:66](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/machines/machine.c:66), [deassert check:135](C:/DocumentNoSnc/CC/GXEMUL/GXEMUL-SEC/src/devices/bus_isa.c:135)
